      "extra": {
        "hover": "【Persian Gulf oil exports remain under pressure as crude prices reprice geopolitical risk】Oil prices steadied early Thursday after rising for two straight sessions, as ongoing military conflict in the Middle East threatened a recovery in regional crude exports and raised concerns over supply disruptions. WTI hovered near $84 a barrel after gaining more than 4% over the previous two sessions. Brent settled below $95. Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia have continued, including on Ras Tanura, a major oil export hub. Iran also said two tankers transiting the Strait of Hormuz struck mines on Nov. 25. “As long as the conflict remains unresolved, crude prices should retain some risk premium,” said Ryan McKay, senior commodity strategist at TD Securities, adding that Persian Gulf oil exports have declined following repeated Iranian attacks. Trading is expected to be thin on Thursday due to the Thanksgiving holiday."
      },
